00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:31.030
I'll either sustain or overrule it.

00:00:31.030 --> 00:00:32.750
And while you're there,
let me know what movie

00:00:32.750 --> 00:00:34.610
or TV show I should do next

00:00:34.610 --> 00:00:35.930
and stick around until the end

00:00:35.930 --> 00:00:39.350
when I give Liar Liar a
grade for legal realism.

00:00:39.350 --> 00:00:40.610
So without further ado,

00:00:40.610 --> 00:00:43.565
let's dig into the
second half of Liar Liar.

00:00:43.565 --> 00:00:47.840
(dramatic orchestral music)

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:50.363
That is not what courtroom
bathrooms look like.

00:00:51.591 --> 00:00:52.733
That is way too nice.

00:00:53.753 --> 00:00:57.960
(mischievous orchestral music)
(people murmuring)

00:00:57.960 --> 00:00:59.640
Why are all these people in this court?

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:02.130
This is a random family divorce.

00:01:02.130 --> 00:01:04.000
There's no reason all those
people should be there.

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:06.440
- From March 6th through June 12th,

00:01:06.440 --> 00:01:10.220
I surveilled Mrs. Cole at
the behest of Mr. Cole.

00:01:10.220 --> 00:01:12.930
During that period, I noted
that Mr. Cole left each day

00:01:12.930 --> 00:01:16.160
between 7:40 and 7:50.

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:20.160
Thereafter Mrs. Cole would
frequently have a male visitor

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:23.630
arrive and stay from one to four hours.

00:01:23.630 --> 00:01:25.780
- So at this point, I
think Fletcher Reede,

00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:28.560
Jim Carrey's character,
should probably be objecting.

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:30.060
There're a couple issues here.

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:31.170
I don't know if they'd win the day,

00:01:31.170 --> 00:01:33.060
but they're probably worth objecting to.

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:35.820
Number one, California
has some really strong

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:37.270
invasion of privacy laws,

00:01:37.270 --> 00:01:41.700
so this investigator is
conceivable invading the privacy

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:45.000
of Mrs. Cole, presumably what is going on

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:47.740
in her own residence
inside of her own bedroom.

00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:51.090
I don't know if that creates
an evidentiary burden

00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:52.970
to bring this evidence in,

00:01:52.970 --> 00:01:55.020
and therefore it would be unlawful

00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:56.480
to bring this kind of evidence

00:01:56.480 --> 00:01:58.090
into their divorce proceedings.

00:01:58.090 --> 00:02:00.470
That doesn't seem like
it would carry the day

00:02:00.470 --> 00:02:02.900
because I'm sure that
there are many instances

00:02:02.900 --> 00:02:05.050
of private investigators
actually taking the stand

00:02:05.050 --> 00:02:07.470
and providing exactly
this kind of evidence

00:02:07.470 --> 00:02:10.260
in a wedding proceeding to show adultery

00:02:10.260 --> 00:02:12.290
in a violation of the
prenuptial agreement.

00:02:12.290 --> 00:02:16.340
But as a putatively
experienced trial lawyer,

00:02:16.340 --> 00:02:18.870
Fletcher Reede really should
be breaking up the flow

00:02:18.870 --> 00:02:20.730
to prevent the private detective

00:02:20.730 --> 00:02:21.790
from getting into a rhythm

00:02:21.790 --> 00:02:23.280
and getting his entire story out

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:25.690
and showing all of
presumably the bad evidence

00:02:25.690 --> 00:02:26.850
that's about to come out.

00:02:26.850 --> 00:02:29.880
- [Investigator] I was able
to take several photographs

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:31.678
of the male visitor.
(Fletcher gulps)

00:02:31.678 --> 00:02:33.261
- [Attorney] I see.

00:02:34.930 --> 00:02:37.170
- I don't know what the
attorney has just done here.

00:02:37.170 --> 00:02:40.050
She has handed the
photographs from the witness

00:02:40.050 --> 00:02:43.290
to the judge without laying the
foundation that's necessary.

00:02:43.290 --> 00:02:45.920
A photograph needs to have foundation laid

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:47.760
before you can bring it into court,

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:48.670
to bring it into evidence,

00:02:48.670 --> 00:02:50.480
and if you don't bring it into evidence,

00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:52.850
then it's as if the
photograph never existed

00:02:52.850 --> 00:02:56.380
because the judge can only
take things into consideration

00:02:56.380 --> 00:02:58.570
that actually came into evidence.

00:02:58.570 --> 00:03:00.280
So she needs to lay the foundation

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:02.910
that this person took those photographs,

00:03:02.910 --> 00:03:05.390
that they reflect the scene accurately

00:03:05.390 --> 00:03:07.110
when he took those photographs,

00:03:07.110 --> 00:03:09.070
and that they're a fair
and accurate representation

00:03:09.070 --> 00:03:11.020
of what actually happened.

00:03:11.020 --> 00:03:13.700
So those are all evidentiary hurdles

00:03:13.700 --> 00:03:16.740
that this witness must
establish and overcome

00:03:16.740 --> 00:03:20.290
in order for these pictures
to be entered into evidence.

00:03:20.290 --> 00:03:21.930
The attorney just can't pick them up

00:03:21.930 --> 00:03:23.490
and then hand them over to the judge.

00:03:23.490 --> 00:03:24.590
That just, it doesn't happen.

00:03:24.590 --> 00:03:26.960
You slowly enter them into evidence

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:29.390
after you've laid the
appropriate foundation.

00:03:29.390 --> 00:03:31.620
- Your Honor, as you are aware,

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:33.630
under the terms of the
prenuptial agreement,

00:03:33.630 --> 00:03:35.640
if Mrs. Cole commits adultery,

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:37.683
- No.
- she is entitled to nothing.

00:03:38.540 --> 00:03:40.520
With your permission, we'd like to play

00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:41.627
the following tape recording.

00:03:41.627 --> 00:03:43.810
(playback button clicking)

00:03:43.810 --> 00:03:46.250
- No, okay, so there are a lot of problems

00:03:46.250 --> 00:03:48.910
with what just happened in court.

00:03:48.910 --> 00:03:51.520
In order to establish that this witness

00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:53.850
has the personal
knowledge of what happened

00:03:53.850 --> 00:03:56.770
between Mrs. Cole and
her gentleman callers,

00:03:56.770 --> 00:04:00.480
the lawyer has to establish
that he has some reason

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:02.010
for this personal knowledge.

00:04:02.010 --> 00:04:04.190
In other words, he has some basis

00:04:04.190 --> 00:04:06.640
to be able to opine as
to what was going on.

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:10.040
And he actually took a step
back and said the wrong thing,

00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:12.530
that he doesn't really
have personal knowledge

00:04:12.530 --> 00:04:14.740
because they kept the shades drawn.

00:04:14.740 --> 00:04:17.020
So he doesn't have a visual confirmation

00:04:17.020 --> 00:04:17.853
of what was going on.

00:04:17.853 --> 00:04:21.590
What he does is that he
heard what was going on

00:04:21.590 --> 00:04:22.760
behind closed doors.

00:04:22.760 --> 00:04:26.100
He was using some form of
electronic surveillance

00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:28.920
to hear what was going on in the bedroom.

00:04:28.920 --> 00:04:32.580
Unless, of course, they were
engaging in conduct so loudly

00:04:32.580 --> 00:04:33.850
that he was able to hear that

00:04:33.850 --> 00:04:36.060
without the aid of an electronic device.

00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:37.530
But let's assume for the moment that this

00:04:37.530 --> 00:04:40.340
is a fairly sophisticated
private investigator

00:04:40.340 --> 00:04:42.610
and he's using electronic
surveillance equipment.

00:04:42.610 --> 00:04:46.650
If that's the case, then he
doesn't have personal knowledge

00:04:46.650 --> 00:04:49.380
except for what is on this video tape.

00:04:49.380 --> 00:04:52.250
Now, California not only
has very strict invasion

00:04:52.250 --> 00:04:56.210
of privacy laws, but
California also has very strict

00:04:56.210 --> 00:04:58.400
electronic surveillance laws.

00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:01.270
And California is a
two-party consent state,

00:05:01.270 --> 00:05:04.180
which means you need to have consent

00:05:04.180 --> 00:05:06.810
from all of the parties to a conversation

00:05:06.810 --> 00:05:09.030
before you're allowed to
electronically surveil them,

00:05:09.030 --> 00:05:11.680
so you can't just pick up the
phone and start recording.

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:14.010
You need to actually get
consent from the other person

00:05:14.010 --> 00:05:15.540
that's on the line of that telephone.

00:05:15.540 --> 00:05:17.650
And electronically surveilling someone

00:05:17.650 --> 00:05:20.730
in their own house might be a violation

00:05:20.730 --> 00:05:23.910
of that electronic
surveillance wiretapping law,

00:05:23.910 --> 00:05:26.020
which I think would really preclude

00:05:26.020 --> 00:05:27.730
this particular evidence.

00:05:27.730 --> 00:05:29.590
So Fletcher Reede really
should have objected

00:05:29.590 --> 00:05:31.080
on multiple grounds right now

00:05:31.080 --> 00:05:34.240
to prevent this kind of
testimony from coming in.

00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:36.020
It might be an invasion of privacy,

00:05:36.020 --> 00:05:39.290
it might be a violation of the
Electronic Surveillance Act,

00:05:39.290 --> 00:05:40.740
and there's very little chance

00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:43.340
that Mrs. Cole consented
to this recording,

00:05:43.340 --> 00:05:46.530
so those are all very
good reasons, by statute,

00:05:46.530 --> 00:05:48.455
that this evidence might not come in.

00:05:48.455 --> 00:05:50.581
- [Samantha] Ah, do it to me.
(man grunting)

00:05:50.581 --> 00:05:53.632
Oh, do it to me, harder, harder.

00:05:53.632 --> 00:05:58.632
(Samantha screaming)
(man grunting)

00:06:00.070 --> 00:06:01.385
- (splutters) Oh, come on!

00:06:01.385 --> 00:06:03.170
(chuckling)

00:06:03.170 --> 00:06:05.820
Okay, there's also a huge hearsay problem,

00:06:05.820 --> 00:06:09.240
and I just live for
good hearsay questions.

00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:13.050
The tape itself is hearsay and arguably,

00:06:13.050 --> 00:06:15.760
what is being said on
this tape is also hearsay,

00:06:15.760 --> 00:06:18.000
so there is a double hearsay problem.

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:20.430
Now, you can get over double hearsay

00:06:20.430 --> 00:06:24.580
by showing an exception or
an exemption at both levels,

00:06:24.580 --> 00:06:26.970
but because there's hearsay
in what's being said

00:06:26.970 --> 00:06:28.870
and hearsay in the form of the tape,

00:06:28.870 --> 00:06:32.050
you have to have an argument
for both of those levels

00:06:32.050 --> 00:06:36.090
before testimony like this,
evidence like this can come in.

00:06:36.090 --> 00:06:38.950
So let's talk about the
first level of hearsay

00:06:38.950 --> 00:06:40.820
in the tape itself.

00:06:40.820 --> 00:06:42.940
Now, just because you have a recording

00:06:42.940 --> 00:06:45.050
does not mean that it's not hearsay.

00:06:45.050 --> 00:06:47.710
On the contrary, it is still
an out-of-court statement,

00:06:47.710 --> 00:06:51.230
a document can be hearsay,
a videotape can be hearsay,

00:06:51.230 --> 00:06:54.020
and an audiotape like this
can definitely be hearsay.

00:06:54.020 --> 00:06:55.340
So I think the easiest way

00:06:55.340 --> 00:06:57.940
to get around this hearsay problem

00:06:57.940 --> 00:07:01.540
is to establish from
the private investigator

00:07:01.540 --> 00:07:03.720
that this is business record.

00:07:03.720 --> 00:07:07.550
He is essentially in the
business of creating audiotapes

00:07:07.550 --> 00:07:10.590
exactly like this, that he
has done it many times before,

00:07:10.590 --> 00:07:14.480
and then lay the elements of
the business records exception,

00:07:14.480 --> 00:07:18.730
that he is a custodian for
this particular audiotape,

00:07:18.730 --> 00:07:20.710
if not the creator of this audiotape,

00:07:20.710 --> 00:07:23.320
and that it was formed in the
regular course of business,

00:07:23.320 --> 00:07:25.310
and just go over the different grounds

00:07:25.310 --> 00:07:27.180
for the business records exception.

00:07:27.180 --> 00:07:30.490
So let's assume that he's able
to get over the first level

00:07:30.490 --> 00:07:32.530
of hearsay in the audiotape itself.

00:07:32.530 --> 00:07:35.620
I think that there is a
presumption that emails

00:07:35.620 --> 00:07:39.220
and actual recordings tend
to be pretty reliable,

00:07:39.220 --> 00:07:41.220
so courts try to bend over backwards

00:07:41.220 --> 00:07:43.490
to get those kind of records in.

00:07:43.490 --> 00:07:46.620
So we get to the second layer of hearsay,

00:07:46.620 --> 00:07:48.680
and here's where it
gets really interesting.

00:07:48.680 --> 00:07:51.340
You might say that what
is going on in this tape,

00:07:51.340 --> 00:07:54.030
they're out-of-court
statements and therefore,

00:07:54.030 --> 00:07:56.330
they should be considered hearsay

00:07:56.330 --> 00:07:58.830
and excludable from evidence.

00:07:58.830 --> 00:08:01.390
But remember that hearsay
is not just any statement.

00:08:01.390 --> 00:08:02.970
It is a statement used

00:08:02.970 --> 00:08:05.690
to prove the truth of the matter asserted.

00:08:05.690 --> 00:08:08.590
Now, no one is using this
tape for the voracity

00:08:08.590 --> 00:08:10.960
of what is actually
being said on the tape.

00:08:10.960 --> 00:08:13.490
In other words, that her gentleman caller

00:08:13.490 --> 00:08:15.890
is actually better in bed than her husband

00:08:15.890 --> 00:08:19.170
or that God exists
because she keeps saying,

00:08:19.170 --> 00:08:20.003
oh, God, oh, God.

00:08:20.003 --> 00:08:23.730
Instead, what they are doing
is showing the existence

00:08:23.730 --> 00:08:25.740
of the underlying acts, in other words,

00:08:25.740 --> 00:08:27.580
that she committed adultery

00:08:27.580 --> 00:08:32.580
and these exclamations are
evidence of engaging in that act,

00:08:33.360 --> 00:08:36.030
which has legal significance
under the prenuptial agreement.

00:08:36.030 --> 00:08:39.760
So I don't think this
actually qualifies as hearsay

00:08:39.760 --> 00:08:42.180
because hearsay must be a statement used

00:08:42.180 --> 00:08:43.540
for the truth of the matter asserted.

00:08:43.540 --> 00:08:45.470
They're not using it for the
truth of the matter asserted,

00:08:45.470 --> 00:08:47.720
and therefore, we don't
even have to talk about

00:08:47.720 --> 00:08:49.670
whether it's an exception
to the hearsay rule.

00:08:49.670 --> 00:08:52.720
I don't think it qualifies as
hearsay in the first instance.

00:08:52.720 --> 00:08:55.450
Now, that being said, I
think Fletcher Reede really

00:08:55.450 --> 00:08:57.010
should be objecting here

00:08:57.010 --> 00:08:58.810
because he needs to break this up,

00:08:58.810 --> 00:09:00.480
he needs to try to prevent this tape

00:09:00.480 --> 00:09:01.750
from coming into evidence,

00:09:01.750 --> 00:09:03.470
and he's just allowed the other attorney

00:09:03.470 --> 00:09:05.540
to play it whenever she wants

00:09:05.540 --> 00:09:07.890
and establish its credibility.

00:09:07.890 --> 00:09:10.620
He might lose these hearsay objections

00:09:10.620 --> 00:09:13.720
because it probably gets
in under the first level

00:09:13.720 --> 00:09:15.800
and it's probably not even
hearsay on the second level,

00:09:15.800 --> 00:09:17.760
but still, you gotta object,

00:09:17.760 --> 00:09:20.680
you've got to preserve the record

00:09:20.680 --> 00:09:21.810
to show that you've objected

00:09:21.810 --> 00:09:24.070
so that you can make
that argument on appeal,

00:09:24.070 --> 00:09:25.570
and you've gotta break up the flow

00:09:25.570 --> 00:09:26.880
of the direct examination.

00:09:26.880 --> 00:09:29.023
Not good lawyering by Fletcher Reede here.

00:09:30.060 --> 00:09:31.930
- Your Honor, I object.

00:09:31.930 --> 00:09:33.600
- And why is that, Mr. Reede?

00:09:33.600 --> 00:09:35.600
- Because it's devastating to my case.

00:09:35.600 --> 00:09:36.600
- Overruled.

00:09:36.600 --> 00:09:37.860
- Good call.

00:09:37.860 --> 00:09:38.900
- Not a valid objection

00:09:38.900 --> 00:09:41.120
and the judge was right to overrule it.

00:09:41.120 --> 00:09:44.580
(dramatic orchestral flare)

00:09:44.580 --> 00:09:45.840
- Your Honor, would the court be willing

00:09:45.840 --> 00:09:48.360
to grant me a short bathroom break ?

00:09:48.360 --> 00:09:49.400
- Can't it wait ?

00:09:49.400 --> 00:09:50.233
- Yes, it can.

00:09:51.330 --> 00:09:52.163
But I've heard that if you hold it,

00:09:52.163 --> 00:09:53.500
it can damage the prostate gland,

00:09:53.500 --> 00:09:55.290
making it very difficult
to get an erection

00:09:55.290 --> 00:09:57.490
or even become aroused.

00:09:57.490 --> 00:09:58.780
- Is that true?

00:09:58.780 --> 00:09:59.683
- It has to be.

00:10:01.200 --> 00:10:04.330
- Well, in that case, I better
take a little break myself.

00:10:04.330 --> 00:10:05.920
- You know, I was going to object

00:10:05.920 --> 00:10:07.890
and say that this is a conversation

00:10:07.890 --> 00:10:10.280
that really should take
place during a sidebar,

00:10:10.280 --> 00:10:12.240
but then it occurred to
me that there is no jury,

00:10:12.240 --> 00:10:14.600
so there's no reason to have a sidebar.

00:10:14.600 --> 00:10:16.870
A sidebar is only for
when you want something

00:10:16.870 --> 00:10:19.950
to be talked about outside
of the presence of the jury,

00:10:19.950 --> 00:10:21.480
and since there is no jury here,

00:10:21.480 --> 00:10:23.640
there's no reason to have a sidebar.

00:10:23.640 --> 00:10:27.040
Examinations are gonna
take hours if not days,

00:10:27.040 --> 00:10:28.960
especially a witness as important

00:10:28.960 --> 00:10:30.660
as this private investigator.

00:10:30.660 --> 00:10:33.770
You've gotta lay really,
really specific foundation,

00:10:33.770 --> 00:10:35.350
which they haven't done here,

00:10:35.350 --> 00:10:38.240
so it wouldn't be
surprising for the attorney

00:10:38.240 --> 00:10:40.480
to ask for a short recess

00:10:40.480 --> 00:10:42.420
before starting into
their cross-examination

00:10:42.420 --> 00:10:45.733
of one of the most important
witnesses of the entire trial.

00:10:45.733 --> 00:10:47.074
(frantic orchestral music)

00:10:47.074 --> 00:10:48.506
(grunting)

00:10:48.506 --> 00:10:50.758
(growling)

00:10:50.758 --> 00:10:52.712
(grunting)

00:10:52.712 --> 00:10:53.915
(roaring)
(radiator clanging)

00:10:53.915 --> 00:10:57.165
- Oh, ah, ah! (crying)

00:11:00.115 --> 00:11:03.057
- What the hell are you doing?

00:11:03.057 --> 00:11:06.923
- I'm kicking my ass, do you mind?

00:11:09.590 --> 00:11:12.110
- Fletcher Reede is really gonna
need a new suit after this.

00:11:12.110 --> 00:11:13.720
If you need a new suit,
check out the links

00:11:13.720 --> 00:11:14.660
in the description below.

00:11:14.660 --> 00:11:17.870
It's where I get all my suits
and it helps out the channel.

00:11:17.870 --> 00:11:18.703
- Order!

00:11:18.703 --> 00:11:19.536
(people murmuring)

00:11:19.536 --> 00:11:20.369
Order!

00:11:20.369 --> 00:11:23.070
(gavel pounding)

00:11:25.240 --> 00:11:27.400
Under the circumstances, I have no choice

00:11:27.400 --> 00:11:31.580
but to recess this case until
9:00 a.m. tomorrow morning.

00:11:31.580 --> 00:11:34.573
Unless, of course, you
feel you can still proceed.

00:11:36.136 --> 00:11:38.450
(mischievous flute music)

00:11:38.450 --> 00:11:42.420
- So, in reality, if someone
really had been beaten up

00:11:42.420 --> 00:11:45.090
in the middle of trial,
everything would stop.

00:11:45.090 --> 00:11:47.510
There would basically
be a manhunt going on

00:11:47.510 --> 00:11:48.740
in this courthouse.

00:11:48.740 --> 00:11:51.080
The judge would never just ask

00:11:51.080 --> 00:11:52.930
if Fletcher Reede was ready to continue

00:11:52.930 --> 00:11:55.730
because there would be police
swarming all over the place,

00:11:55.730 --> 00:11:57.740
maybe the SWAT team was called in.

00:11:57.740 --> 00:11:59.150
There's gonna be an investigation

00:11:59.150 --> 00:12:01.270
and they need to find this horrible person

00:12:01.270 --> 00:12:03.730
that presumably beat the
hell out of Fletcher Reede,

00:12:03.730 --> 00:12:07.660
so this trial would not
continue on the same day at all.

00:12:07.660 --> 00:12:09.113
It would stop immediately.

00:12:10.922 --> 00:12:12.429
(phone ringing)

00:12:12.429 --> 00:12:14.596
(sighing)

00:12:17.046 --> 00:12:18.690
- Hello?

00:12:18.690 --> 00:12:20.430
- So you actually have
to be really careful

00:12:20.430 --> 00:12:22.100
when you bring cellphones into court.

00:12:22.100 --> 00:12:24.230
Some courthouses allow it, but others,

00:12:24.230 --> 00:12:26.590
like the federal courthouse in Virginia

00:12:26.590 --> 00:12:29.160
that proceeded over the Manafort case,

00:12:29.160 --> 00:12:31.430
don't allow any cellphones whatsoever,

00:12:31.430 --> 00:12:34.290
and they don't even have cubbies or safes

00:12:34.290 --> 00:12:36.550
to lock up your cellphone
when you get there.

00:12:36.550 --> 00:12:38.020
So if you arrive in court,

00:12:38.020 --> 00:12:40.210
you're forced to get rid of your cellphone

00:12:40.210 --> 00:12:43.460
or find some other place
off-premises to store it.

00:12:43.460 --> 00:12:45.850
God help you if your cellphone goes off

00:12:45.850 --> 00:12:46.880
and the judge hears it.

00:12:46.880 --> 00:12:49.600
I have seen people expelled from court

00:12:49.600 --> 00:12:52.050
specifically for violating
a rule like that.

00:12:52.050 --> 00:12:54.530
Judges can be really, really persnickety

00:12:54.530 --> 00:12:56.320
when it comes to electronic devices

00:12:56.320 --> 00:12:58.810
and things going off in their courtroom,

00:12:58.810 --> 00:13:00.260
so you gotta be very careful.

00:13:02.530 --> 00:13:03.750
- Respondent calls

00:13:06.510 --> 00:13:07.733
Kenneth Falk.

00:13:08.750 --> 00:13:11.610
- I think that's right, to
call them the respondent.

00:13:11.610 --> 00:13:13.070
This is not a situation where you'd have

00:13:13.070 --> 00:13:14.820
plaintiff and defendant.

00:13:14.820 --> 00:13:17.440
I think it would be
claimant and respondent,

00:13:17.440 --> 00:13:18.580
which is a nice little touch

00:13:18.580 --> 00:13:19.860
to get that terminology right.

00:13:19.860 --> 00:13:21.113
I think that's correct.

00:13:26.660 --> 00:13:27.493
- Mr. Falk.
- Don't do it,

00:13:27.493 --> 00:13:28.483
don't enter the well.

00:13:33.030 --> 00:13:34.980
- Do you know my client, Samantha Cole?

00:13:35.970 --> 00:13:36.803
- Yes.

00:13:36.803 --> 00:13:39.660
- Okay, good question,
establishing, open-ended.

00:13:39.660 --> 00:13:40.493
Good job.

00:13:40.493 --> 00:13:42.300
- Isn't it true that your relationship

00:13:42.300 --> 00:13:45.040
with my client is entirely platonic?

00:13:45.040 --> 00:13:46.673
I object, Your Honor!

00:13:47.520 --> 00:13:48.583
- To yourself?

00:13:50.324 --> 00:13:51.800
- Yeah.

00:13:51.800 --> 00:13:52.750
But I would like to

00:13:54.320 --> 00:13:56.054
rephrase the question.

00:13:56.054 --> 00:13:57.360
- Ah.

00:13:57.360 --> 00:13:58.750
- That was actually a good objection.

00:13:58.750 --> 00:14:01.750
He asked a leading
question of this witness

00:14:01.750 --> 00:14:05.030
and if he hadn't objected,
the other attorney should have

00:14:05.030 --> 00:14:07.970
because you're not allowed
to lead your own witness.

00:14:07.970 --> 00:14:09.320
This is direct examination.

00:14:09.320 --> 00:14:11.240
All your questions have to be open-ended,

00:14:11.240 --> 00:14:14.400
so when he asked, isn't
it true that you've never,

00:14:14.400 --> 00:14:16.270
blah, blah, blah, that was improper.

00:14:16.270 --> 00:14:19.470
That question implied only one answer,

00:14:19.470 --> 00:14:21.300
and that's exactly what
you're not allowed to do

00:14:21.300 --> 00:14:22.780
on direct examination.

00:14:22.780 --> 00:14:25.520
So good objection and good
striking of the record.

00:14:25.520 --> 00:14:27.270
- You had sex with her every time you met,

00:14:27.270 --> 00:14:28.694
didn't you, didn't you?

00:14:28.694 --> 00:14:29.590
Liar!

00:14:29.590 --> 00:14:30.683
- He's badgering the witness.

00:14:30.683 --> 00:14:31.790
- It's his witness.

00:14:31.790 --> 00:14:32.623
- You slammed her!

00:14:32.623 --> 00:14:33.543
You dunked her donuts!
- Still can't badger

00:14:33.543 --> 00:14:34.376
the witness.
- You gave her dog

00:14:34.376 --> 00:14:35.770
a snaussage, you stuffed her

00:14:35.770 --> 00:14:37.561
like a Thanksgiving turkey!

00:14:37.561 --> 00:14:40.450
(gobbling)

00:14:40.450 --> 00:14:42.540
- All right, all right, it's true, okay?

00:14:42.540 --> 00:14:43.670
I humped her brains out!

00:14:43.670 --> 00:14:44.933
There, now you happy?

00:14:51.570 --> 00:14:52.566
- No further questions.

00:14:52.566 --> 00:14:54.860
(chuckling)

00:14:54.860 --> 00:14:56.810
- So I probably don't need to tell you

00:14:56.810 --> 00:14:58.740
that there's all kinds of
things that are wrong here.

00:14:58.740 --> 00:15:01.560
Number one, he's asking
tons of leading questions,

00:15:01.560 --> 00:15:04.040
he is badgering the witness like crazy,

00:15:04.040 --> 00:15:06.880
he is asking argumentative questions.

00:15:06.880 --> 00:15:09.120
Just because he has called this witness

00:15:09.120 --> 00:15:10.820
doesn't mean that he is allowed to engage

00:15:10.820 --> 00:15:12.170
in that kind of behavior.

00:15:12.170 --> 00:15:13.400
That's totally unprofessional

00:15:13.400 --> 00:15:15.450
and it's really probably a violation

00:15:15.450 --> 00:15:18.250
of your duty of loyalty to your client,

00:15:18.250 --> 00:15:19.970
so despite him being honest,

00:15:19.970 --> 00:15:23.750
he is still breaking his
ethical rules to his client.

00:15:23.750 --> 00:15:25.540
So what he really needs
to do is just shut up

00:15:25.540 --> 00:15:26.760
and not say anything.

00:15:26.760 --> 00:15:28.870
That way, he can both tell the truth

00:15:28.870 --> 00:15:32.423
and also uphold his ethical
obligations to his client.

00:15:33.426 --> 00:15:34.259
- Uh.

00:15:36.810 --> 00:15:37.740
No questions.

00:15:37.740 --> 00:15:39.020
- Yeah, exactly right.

00:15:39.020 --> 00:15:41.880
When you don't need to gild the lily,

00:15:41.880 --> 00:15:44.550
you don't need to get anything
else out of the witness,

00:15:44.550 --> 00:15:46.440
you do not ask any questions,

00:15:46.440 --> 00:15:48.250
you do not ask any followup.

00:15:48.250 --> 00:15:50.780
You just let the record remain where it is

00:15:50.780 --> 00:15:52.980
so that the other side
doesn't have an opportunity

00:15:52.980 --> 00:15:56.510
to have a rebuttal or to do damage control

00:15:56.510 --> 00:15:57.700
of what they have already done.

00:15:57.700 --> 00:16:00.090
You just let sleeping
dogs lie and you move on,

00:16:00.090 --> 00:16:02.600
so great job by this other attorney.

00:16:02.600 --> 00:16:06.470
- Is this this a copy of
your driver's license?

00:16:06.470 --> 00:16:07.860
- Not allowed to enter the well like that.

00:16:07.860 --> 00:16:09.357
The bailiff will tackle you.

00:16:09.357 --> 00:16:11.630
- And it says here you were born in 1964.

00:16:11.630 --> 00:16:14.000
But that's not true either, is it?

00:16:14.000 --> 00:16:14.833
Is it!

00:16:14.833 --> 00:16:15.890
- No.

00:16:15.890 --> 00:16:17.060
- Will you tell me what it says here

00:16:17.060 --> 00:16:20.010
on your birth certificate
under date of birth?

00:16:20.010 --> 00:16:21.072
- Your Honor, I object.

00:16:21.072 --> 00:16:22.660
What does this have to do with anything?

00:16:22.660 --> 00:16:23.890
- Overruled.

00:16:23.890 --> 00:16:25.350
- None of this stuff is in evidence yet.

00:16:25.350 --> 00:16:27.110
You have to add this to the evidence

00:16:27.110 --> 00:16:28.570
before you're allowed to talk about it.

00:16:28.570 --> 00:16:30.700
You just need to establish
that a material fact

00:16:30.700 --> 00:16:33.030
is more or less likely to be true

00:16:33.030 --> 00:16:36.540
based on the presence of
this evidence or testimony,

00:16:36.540 --> 00:16:38.740
so that's a very, very low bar.

00:16:38.740 --> 00:16:41.710
So in reality, if the
judge didn't really know

00:16:41.710 --> 00:16:44.080
where Fletcher Reede was
going with this argument,

00:16:44.080 --> 00:16:46.730
then what the judge would
do is ask Fletcher Reede

00:16:46.730 --> 00:16:48.020
to make a proffer.

00:16:48.020 --> 00:16:50.670
He would not just say, I have
no idea what's going to happen

00:16:50.670 --> 00:16:53.270
so I'm just going to allow
this evidence to come in.

00:16:53.270 --> 00:16:56.190
The idea that you're
gonna sandbag everyone,

00:16:56.190 --> 00:16:58.340
including the judge and the other side,

00:16:58.340 --> 00:17:00.160
it rarely happens in real life.

00:17:00.160 --> 00:17:00.993
There's so much briefing

00:17:00.993 --> 00:17:02.420
and there's so much back and forth

00:17:02.420 --> 00:17:04.820
that you have an idea of what arguments

00:17:04.820 --> 00:17:06.350
are going to be made ahead of time.

00:17:06.350 --> 00:17:08.750
It's not just sprung
on you and your client

00:17:08.750 --> 00:17:10.204
like it is in this movie.

00:17:10.204 --> 00:17:14.203
- And the truth shall set you free!

00:17:15.430 --> 00:17:17.330
My client lied about her age!

00:17:17.330 --> 00:17:19.260
She was only 17 when she got married,

00:17:19.260 --> 00:17:21.070
which makes her a minor.

00:17:21.070 --> 00:17:22.790
And in the great state of California,

00:17:22.790 --> 00:17:25.400
no minor can enter into any legal contract

00:17:25.400 --> 00:17:28.530
without parental consent, including...

00:17:28.530 --> 00:17:29.620
- Prenuptial agreements.

00:17:29.620 --> 00:17:31.500
- Prenuptial agreements!

00:17:31.500 --> 00:17:33.215
This contract is void!

00:17:33.215 --> 00:17:34.120
- Voidable.
- The fact that my client

00:17:34.120 --> 00:17:36.830
has been ridden more than
Seattle Slew is irrelevant.

00:17:36.830 --> 00:17:38.360
- Sort of.
- Standard community property

00:17:38.360 --> 00:17:40.630
applies, and she is entitled
to half of the marital assets.

00:17:40.630 --> 00:17:43.830
- Maybe.
- All $11.395 million.

00:17:43.830 --> 00:17:46.810
Jordan fades back, swoosh,
and that's the game!

00:17:46.810 --> 00:17:48.460
- That is not the game.

00:17:48.460 --> 00:17:51.210
- Nothing further, Your Honor.

00:17:51.210 --> 00:17:54.730
- All right, let's talk
about marriage law.

00:17:54.730 --> 00:17:58.340
Last time I covered this
scene just in isolation.

00:17:58.340 --> 00:18:00.330
I pointed out that in California,

00:18:00.330 --> 00:18:03.940
a minor can enter into a contract

00:18:03.940 --> 00:18:05.650
when they are still a minor,

00:18:05.650 --> 00:18:07.690
that that contract becomes voidable

00:18:07.690 --> 00:18:09.810
at the option of the minor,

00:18:09.810 --> 00:18:12.900
who can then ratify or
disclaim that contract

00:18:12.900 --> 00:18:14.770
when they get to the age of majority,

00:18:14.770 --> 00:18:17.570
which is the age of 18 in California.

00:18:17.570 --> 00:18:19.400
So as a technical matter,

00:18:19.400 --> 00:18:22.100
the contract is not void ab initio.

00:18:22.100 --> 00:18:23.240
In other words, it's not void

00:18:23.240 --> 00:18:25.070
from the very beginning of the contract.

00:18:25.070 --> 00:18:28.630
It is voidable at the option of the minor.

00:18:28.630 --> 00:18:31.130
But that is not the end of the question.

00:18:31.130 --> 00:18:34.072
Now it's time to think like a lawyer.

00:18:34.072 --> 00:18:37.170
(rock music)

00:18:37.170 --> 00:18:40.860
So the respondent argues
that the prenuptial agreement

00:18:40.860 --> 00:18:45.160
is voidable and she's exercising
her option to void it.

00:18:45.160 --> 00:18:48.100
All right, so what does
the other side say?

00:18:48.100 --> 00:18:49.410
Well, this is not the end of the game,

00:18:49.410 --> 00:18:51.270
this is actually the very beginning.

00:18:51.270 --> 00:18:53.890
What the husband would probably
argue is a couple of things.

00:18:53.890 --> 00:18:58.890
Number one, a contract can be
ratified without an express,

00:18:59.040 --> 00:19:02.640
or in other words, the actual
language of ratification.

00:19:02.640 --> 00:19:05.320
You can ratify a contract by your conduct.

00:19:05.320 --> 00:19:09.890
So the husband would argue
that for the last 13 years,

00:19:09.890 --> 00:19:11.820
this woman has been in the marriage,

00:19:11.820 --> 00:19:14.530
has received all the
benefits of the marriage,

00:19:14.530 --> 00:19:17.290
and therefore has ratified
the prenuptial agreement

00:19:17.290 --> 00:19:19.660
that she entered into
when she was a minor.

00:19:19.660 --> 00:19:22.820
Furthermore, I think the
husband would also argue

00:19:22.820 --> 00:19:26.850
that the defense of fraud applies.

00:19:26.850 --> 00:19:30.740
This woman, without notifying the husband

00:19:30.740 --> 00:19:33.840
or fiance at that time,
fraudulently stated

00:19:33.840 --> 00:19:37.610
that she was of the age
to enter into a marriage

00:19:37.610 --> 00:19:39.610
and a marriage contract.

00:19:39.610 --> 00:19:42.460
So she committed a fraud on the
marriage and on the husband,

00:19:42.460 --> 00:19:45.070
so he would have the burden to establish

00:19:45.070 --> 00:19:48.850
that a fraud was committed
and that is a defense

00:19:48.850 --> 00:19:51.830
to the lack of contract
formation that she's arguing.

00:19:51.830 --> 00:19:54.767
I think he would be likely
to win that defense,

00:19:54.767 --> 00:19:56.450
that affirmative defense.

00:19:56.450 --> 00:19:59.250
Now, the last time I
covered this specific scene,

00:19:59.250 --> 00:20:01.550
a lot of astute viewers pointed out that,

00:20:01.550 --> 00:20:04.770
well, if the prenuptial agreement is void,

00:20:04.770 --> 00:20:07.410
then doesn't that mean that
the entire marriage is void

00:20:07.410 --> 00:20:08.290
from the very beginning?

00:20:08.290 --> 00:20:10.120
Because the same fraud

00:20:10.120 --> 00:20:12.080
that precludes the prenuptial agreement

00:20:12.080 --> 00:20:15.670
from being effective would
prevent the marriage itself

00:20:15.670 --> 00:20:16.503
from being effective?

00:20:16.503 --> 00:20:19.450
Well, that is a very,
very interesting question,

00:20:19.450 --> 00:20:22.720
but that probably doesn't change
the result, and here's why.

00:20:22.720 --> 00:20:26.680
In California, there's no such
thing as common law marriage,

00:20:26.680 --> 00:20:30.340
but what there is is something
called a Marvin claim.

00:20:30.340 --> 00:20:32.760
You might have heard of this as palimony.

00:20:32.760 --> 00:20:35.690
In other words, just
because you are able to show

00:20:35.690 --> 00:20:37.530
that a marriage is invalid,

00:20:37.530 --> 00:20:39.510
there is still a claim to be made

00:20:39.510 --> 00:20:43.010
for essentially exactly the
same rights you would have

00:20:43.010 --> 00:20:45.950
if you had entered into a marriage.

00:20:45.950 --> 00:20:49.600
And the Marvin claim dates back
to, I believe it's the '70s.

00:20:49.600 --> 00:20:52.100
It's named after the actor, Lee Marvin,

00:20:52.100 --> 00:20:55.800
who had a seven-year
relationship with a woman

00:20:55.800 --> 00:20:57.610
and they didn't get married,

00:20:57.610 --> 00:21:01.970
but the court found that even
in the absence of a marriage,

00:21:01.970 --> 00:21:05.900
the equities required
some division of assets

00:21:05.900 --> 00:21:10.370
along the lines of what would
be given under a marriage.

00:21:10.370 --> 00:21:12.840
I think the equities
in this particular case

00:21:12.840 --> 00:21:15.670
show that there is something
that's like a marriage

00:21:15.670 --> 00:21:20.020
and in that circumstance, the
marriage rules would kick in

00:21:20.020 --> 00:21:21.870
and you would still need to decide

00:21:21.870 --> 00:21:24.690
how those marital assets are divided up.

00:21:24.690 --> 00:21:27.210
California is a community property state.

00:21:27.210 --> 00:21:29.820
It is also a no-fault divorce state,

00:21:29.820 --> 00:21:31.420
which means that in general,

00:21:31.420 --> 00:21:34.660
it doesn't matter who
has engaged in adultery.

00:21:34.660 --> 00:21:37.210
Either party is allowed
to exit the marriage

00:21:37.210 --> 00:21:40.480
whenever they want and if there
is no prenuptial agreement,

00:21:40.480 --> 00:21:41.950
then community property applies

00:21:41.950 --> 00:21:45.820
and there's more or less an
even division of those assets

00:21:45.820 --> 00:21:47.570
that exist in the marital form.

00:21:47.570 --> 00:21:49.690
But to tie this all together,

00:21:49.690 --> 00:21:52.420
I think all the equities
go to the husband,

00:21:52.420 --> 00:21:55.250
and that's something that
courts really do take a look at.

00:21:55.250 --> 00:21:57.810
Even if you have good
arguments on either side

00:21:57.810 --> 00:21:59.190
and there's no clear answer,

00:21:59.190 --> 00:22:01.740
the courts will look to
the equities and here,

00:22:01.740 --> 00:22:05.260
the wife has committed
a fraud on the marriage,

00:22:05.260 --> 00:22:06.840
she's the one that committed the adultery

00:22:06.840 --> 00:22:08.770
in violation of the prenuptial agreement,

00:22:08.770 --> 00:22:10.040
and I think that the court

00:22:10.040 --> 00:22:12.370
is likely to take all of
that into consideration,

00:22:12.370 --> 00:22:15.700
probably find that she
has ratified the contract

00:22:15.700 --> 00:22:18.620
or that the affirmative
defense of fraud applies.

00:22:18.620 --> 00:22:20.550
I think the husband probably wins here.

00:22:20.550 --> 00:22:23.510
But if you disagree, if there
are any family law lawyers

00:22:23.510 --> 00:22:25.950
in the audience, let me
know in the comments below.

00:22:25.950 --> 00:22:27.960
I would love to continue this discussion.

00:22:27.960 --> 00:22:30.260
And by the way, if this
makes your head spin,

00:22:30.260 --> 00:22:31.500
it's you and me both.

00:22:31.500 --> 00:22:34.080
These are the kinds of
gray areas that we get into

00:22:34.080 --> 00:22:35.610
in the law all the time.

00:22:35.610 --> 00:22:38.180
And that's why you often
need to hire a lawyer

00:22:38.180 --> 00:22:39.780
to handle these kinds of things

00:22:39.780 --> 00:22:42.453
because it gets really, really messy.

00:22:43.290 --> 00:22:46.100
- In light of this new
evidence, the court must rule

00:22:46.100 --> 00:22:47.600
in favor of Mrs. Cole.

00:22:47.600 --> 00:22:48.840
She is hereby awarded
- Yes!

00:22:48.840 --> 00:22:51.327
- half the marital assets.

00:22:51.327 --> 00:22:53.770
(gavel banging)
- Again, the judge would never

00:22:53.770 --> 00:22:56.970
just blurt out in the middle
of the trial proceedings

00:22:56.970 --> 00:23:00.320
that one side has won, is
entitled to whatever it is

00:23:00.320 --> 00:23:01.280
that they're asking for.

00:23:01.280 --> 00:23:03.570
Not all the witnesses have been called,

00:23:03.570 --> 00:23:06.180
the parties haven't conducted
their closing arguments,

00:23:06.180 --> 00:23:08.650
they haven't filed
their post-trial briefs,

00:23:08.650 --> 00:23:10.770
they haven't made their
post-trial motions.

00:23:10.770 --> 00:23:13.060
There are a million
things that need to happen

00:23:13.060 --> 00:23:16.030
between now and when the judge
renders his final verdict,

00:23:16.030 --> 00:23:18.160
including giving a tentative decision

00:23:18.160 --> 00:23:21.530
and allowing the parties to
talk about that final decision,

00:23:21.530 --> 00:23:24.250
probably calling for another
settlement conference.

00:23:24.250 --> 00:23:27.960
No way would the judge ever
just bang his gavel and say,

00:23:27.960 --> 00:23:29.480
you win, respondent.

00:23:29.480 --> 00:23:30.610
Not gonna happen.

00:23:30.610 --> 00:23:31.443
- What?

00:23:31.443 --> 00:23:33.120
- If I get sole custody of the kids,

00:23:33.120 --> 00:23:35.780
that's another 10 grand
in child support payments.

00:23:35.780 --> 00:23:37.620
- You just won 11 million dollars.

00:23:37.620 --> 00:23:39.320
- Hey, I'm the victim here.

00:23:39.320 --> 00:23:40.758
You said it yourself.

00:23:40.758 --> 00:23:42.683
And I'm gonna hit him where it hurts.

00:23:44.650 --> 00:23:46.540
- But you...

00:23:46.540 --> 00:23:49.240
You said he was a good father.

00:23:49.240 --> 00:23:50.073
- So?

00:23:52.340 --> 00:23:55.550
- Well, Mr. Reede, do we have an agreement

00:23:55.550 --> 00:23:57.073
on custody or not?

00:23:59.100 --> 00:24:00.840
- I think here, Fletcher Reede needs

00:24:00.840 --> 00:24:03.480
to follow the wishes of his client

00:24:03.480 --> 00:24:07.350
and reject the settlement offer,
although this would, again,

00:24:07.350 --> 00:24:09.280
happen in settlement negotiations

00:24:09.280 --> 00:24:11.360
outside of right in front of the judge

00:24:11.360 --> 00:24:14.130
after having handed down
a verdict in this case.

00:24:14.130 --> 00:24:16.570
But if he finds himself ethically unable

00:24:16.570 --> 00:24:19.010
to make the argument
that she wants to make,

00:24:19.010 --> 00:24:21.420
then he can withdraw from counsel,

00:24:21.420 --> 00:24:23.330
he can talk about
irreconcilable differences

00:24:23.330 --> 00:24:26.420
between him and his client
and make a motion to withdraw,

00:24:26.420 --> 00:24:30.573
as her first divorce attorney
has already done in this case.

00:24:31.997 --> 00:24:34.730
She's not in a bad spot, she
can get another counselor

00:24:34.730 --> 00:24:37.430
to help with this post-trial briefing

00:24:37.430 --> 00:24:40.330
in this post-trial custody dispute.

00:24:40.330 --> 00:24:42.730
So it wouldn't really be
much prejudice to her.

00:24:42.730 --> 00:24:44.870
- Why should you be any different?

00:24:44.870 --> 00:24:46.300
- Bailiff, take him away.

00:24:46.300 --> 00:24:48.650
- This man is a good father!
- I don't think the judge

00:24:48.650 --> 00:24:51.430
has the power to issue a contempt ruling

00:24:51.430 --> 00:24:52.560
when court's not in session.

00:24:52.560 --> 00:24:54.990
This is after the trial has happened.

00:24:54.990 --> 00:24:56.430
I'm not sure he has that power.

00:24:56.430 --> 00:24:57.837
- I'm Jose Canseco!

00:24:58.807 --> 00:25:00.980
I'm Jose Canseco!

00:25:02.206 --> 00:25:03.785
(brash orchestral music)

00:25:03.785 --> 00:25:07.900
(lighthearted orchestral music)

00:25:07.900 --> 00:25:11.672
- Now it's time to give Liar
Liar a grade for legal realism.

00:25:11.672 --> 00:25:13.200
(gavel banging)

00:25:13.200 --> 00:25:16.690
Now, there's a lot going against
it, unrealistic dialogue,

00:25:16.690 --> 00:25:20.410
supernatural elements, totally
unrealistic trial timeline.

00:25:20.410 --> 00:25:22.250
But I have to admit, it was a ton of fun

00:25:22.250 --> 00:25:24.710
to try and untangle this legal web

00:25:24.710 --> 00:25:27.620
involving the prenup and a
minor entering into a contract.

00:25:27.620 --> 00:25:28.970
Good job by the writers.

00:25:28.970 --> 00:25:31.280
So I give Liar Liar the
benefit of the doubt

00:25:31.280 --> 00:25:33.430
and I give it another B plus.

00:25:33.430 --> 00:25:35.270
Not bad, good job, guys.

00:25:35.270 --> 00:25:36.420
Do you agree with that grade?

00:25:36.420 --> 00:25:38.220
Leave your objections
in the comments below

00:25:38.220 --> 00:25:40.170
and let me know what I should review next.

00:25:40.170 --> 00:25:41.610
In the meantime, check out this playlist

00:25:41.610 --> 00:25:43.330
that includes all of my prior reactions,

00:25:43.330 --> 00:25:46.160
including Suits, Better
Call Saul, and tons more.

00:25:46.160 --> 00:25:48.853
So click on the playlist
and I'll see you in court.

